1
00:00:00,061 --> 00:00:02,286
In the next eight minutes, I'll explain why

2
00:00:02,286 --> 00:00:04,586
you stay up when you're exhausted, and hand

3
00:00:04,586 --> 00:00:06,439
you three fixes for tonight.

4
00:00:06,750 --> 00:00:08,175
But first, a question.

5
00:00:08,175 --> 00:00:09,150
You're tired.

6
00:00:09,163 --> 00:00:10,575
You know you're tired.

7
00:00:10,588 --> 00:00:13,188
So why does eleven PM feel like the

8
00:00:13,188 --> 00:00:14,686
best part of your day?

9
00:00:14,840 --> 00:00:15,790
Here's the secret.

10
00:00:15,790 --> 00:00:17,362
It isn't that you hate sleep.

11
00:00:17,494 --> 00:00:19,472
You've been borrowing from tomorrow Bob.

12
00:00:19,654 --> 00:00:22,204
He's the guy you hand every problem to.

13
00:00:22,354 --> 00:00:25,307
Groggy, grumpy, and never once consulted.

14
00:00:25,683 --> 00:00:26,960
He is not a fan.

15
00:00:27,145 --> 00:00:29,295
First, this has an actual name.

16
00:00:29,295 --> 00:00:31,534
And it's less dramatic than you'd hope.

17
00:00:32,060 --> 00:00:34,985
It's called bedtime procrastination.

18
00:00:35,022 --> 00:00:37,810
Researchers define it as going to bed later

19
00:00:37,810 --> 00:00:40,560
than you meant to, with no outside reason

20
00:00:40,560 --> 00:00:41,575
forcing you.

21
00:00:41,774 --> 00:00:44,286
Kroese and colleagues wrote about it in two

22
00:00:44,286 --> 00:00:45,849
thousand fourteen.

23
00:00:45,886 --> 00:00:48,862
The "no outside reason" part is the key.

24
00:00:49,055 --> 00:00:49,918
No deadline.

25
00:00:49,918 --> 00:00:50,730
No newborn.

26
00:00:50,730 --> 00:00:51,743
No night shift.

27
00:00:51,743 --> 00:00:54,005
Just you, a phone, and a very flexible

28
00:00:54,005 --> 00:00:55,830
idea of "ten more minutes."

29
00:00:56,138 --> 00:00:57,713
That's what makes it odd.

30
00:00:57,738 --> 00:01:00,013
Nothing is actually stopping you from going to

31
00:01:00,013 --> 00:01:00,616
bed.

32
00:01:00,772 --> 00:01:02,435
Except the part of you that doesn't want

33
00:01:02,435 --> 00:01:03,014
to.

34
00:01:03,221 --> 00:01:05,045
So it's not a character flaw.

35
00:01:05,071 --> 00:01:07,768
It's a pattern, and patterns have causes.

36
00:01:07,944 --> 00:01:09,056
Honest caveat.

37
00:01:09,056 --> 00:01:11,018
Not every late night is this.

38
00:01:11,081 --> 00:01:13,744
Shift work, young kids, and sleep problems are

39
00:01:13,744 --> 00:01:14,936
different stories.

40
00:01:15,105 --> 00:01:17,618
Now let's talk about why your evening suddenly

41
00:01:17,618 --> 00:01:20,068
feels like the only time that belongs to

42
00:01:20,068 --> 00:01:20,544
you.

43
00:01:21,136 --> 00:01:22,360
Think about your day.

44
00:01:22,373 --> 00:01:23,911
Work owns the morning.

45
00:01:23,936 --> 00:01:25,636
Chores own the afternoon.

46
00:01:25,648 --> 00:01:28,005
People who need things own the evening.

47
00:01:28,184 --> 00:01:30,709
By ten PM, you finally have a few

48
00:01:30,709 --> 00:01:33,559
hours with nobody asking for anything.

49
00:01:33,559 --> 00:01:36,003
It feels like unclaimed land.

50
00:01:36,193 --> 00:01:37,318
So you plant a flag.

51
00:01:37,368 --> 00:01:38,868
"This hour is mine."

52
00:01:38,893 --> 00:01:39,918
Nobody may touch it.

53
00:01:39,918 --> 00:01:41,006
Not even sleep.

54
00:01:41,199 --> 00:01:46,024
Online, people nicknamed this revenge bedtime procrastination.

55
00:01:46,024 --> 00:01:49,112
It's an informal internet nickname, not a medical

56
00:01:49,112 --> 00:01:49,788
term.

57
00:01:49,938 --> 00:01:52,163
Also, "revenge" is a bit dramatic.

58
00:01:52,175 --> 00:01:53,088
Against whom?

59
00:01:53,088 --> 00:01:54,405
Your alarm clock?

60
00:01:54,547 --> 00:01:56,922
The feeling underneath is real, though.

61
00:01:56,960 --> 00:01:58,997
You want some time that isn't owed to

62
00:01:58,997 --> 00:01:59,750
anyone.

63
00:01:59,931 --> 00:02:01,356
The catch is the price.

64
00:02:01,356 --> 00:02:03,606
You pay with sleep, and tomorrow Bob gets

65
00:02:03,606 --> 00:02:04,273
the bill.

66
00:02:04,550 --> 00:02:07,189
Interest rate: one extremely grumpy morning.

67
00:02:07,341 --> 00:02:08,916
But why can't you just stop?

68
00:02:08,916 --> 00:02:10,116
That's a brain problem.

69
00:02:10,116 --> 00:02:11,185
Let's go there.

70
00:02:11,767 --> 00:02:13,216
Here's the cruel part.

71
00:02:13,229 --> 00:02:16,292
By night, self-control tends to run low.

72
00:02:16,329 --> 00:02:19,417
Researchers argue about exactly how that works, but

73
00:02:19,417 --> 00:02:20,382
you've felt it.

74
00:02:20,555 --> 00:02:22,180
It's like a phone battery.

75
00:02:22,180 --> 00:02:23,704
At noon, full.

76
00:02:23,705 --> 00:02:25,792
At midnight, it's at one percent, and you're

77
00:02:25,792 --> 00:02:27,071
opening a game.

78
00:02:27,210 --> 00:02:29,435
The same brain that said no to dessert

79
00:02:29,435 --> 00:02:31,799
at lunch now says yes to everything.

80
00:02:31,947 --> 00:02:33,224
And then there's timing.

81
00:02:33,387 --> 00:02:35,137
Scrolling pays off right now.

82
00:02:35,187 --> 00:02:36,840
Sleep pays off tomorrow.

83
00:02:36,988 --> 00:02:40,037
People tend to favor rewards that come sooner.

84
00:02:40,038 --> 00:02:42,926
It's a very common tendency, and bedtime keeps

85
00:02:42,926 --> 00:02:43,953
losing to it.

86
00:02:44,139 --> 00:02:45,864
So the vote is tonight Bob, all of

87
00:02:45,864 --> 00:02:46,314
them.

88
00:02:46,327 --> 00:02:47,736
Tomorrow Bob, none.

89
00:02:48,120 --> 00:02:49,322
He wasn't invited.

90
00:02:49,468 --> 00:02:51,643
It's not that you can't do math.

91
00:02:51,643 --> 00:02:54,190
It's that tomorrow feels like a stranger.

92
00:02:54,361 --> 00:02:56,586
And a feed knows exactly what to do

93
00:02:56,586 --> 00:02:58,064
with a tired brain.

94
00:02:58,661 --> 00:03:00,773
Now put that tired brain in front of

95
00:03:00,773 --> 00:03:02,561
a feed with no bottom.

96
00:03:02,611 --> 00:03:05,586
No last page, no credits, no signal that

97
00:03:05,586 --> 00:03:06,619
says you're done.

98
00:03:06,798 --> 00:03:09,548
And the rewards are unpredictable, so you keep

99
00:03:09,548 --> 00:03:11,148
checking for the good one.

100
00:03:11,248 --> 00:03:13,624
"One more" is just how it works.

101
00:03:13,783 --> 00:03:15,083
Also, screens.

102
00:03:15,095 --> 00:03:18,083
Some research suggests evening light from screens can

103
00:03:18,083 --> 00:03:20,035
nudge sleep later for some people.

104
00:03:20,215 --> 00:03:22,777
The evidence on how big that effect is,

105
00:03:22,777 --> 00:03:23,715
is mixed.

106
00:03:23,752 --> 00:03:25,652
So I'm not saying the glow is the

107
00:03:25,652 --> 00:03:26,343
villain.

108
00:03:26,624 --> 00:03:28,152
I'm saying it's a suspect.

109
00:03:28,358 --> 00:03:30,483
So you end up somewhere strange.

110
00:03:30,521 --> 00:03:32,096
Exhausted, and wired.

111
00:03:32,096 --> 00:03:34,072
Tired, but unable to stop.

112
00:03:34,205 --> 00:03:37,005
Congratulations, it's one AM, and you have zero

113
00:03:37,005 --> 00:03:37,780
regrets.

114
00:03:37,780 --> 00:03:38,483
For now.

115
00:03:38,633 --> 00:03:40,424
Tomorrow Bob will have all of them.

116
00:03:40,819 --> 00:03:41,678
In bulk.

117
00:03:41,836 --> 00:03:43,511
Okay, enough roasting.

118
00:03:43,536 --> 00:03:44,486
Fixes.

119
00:03:44,499 --> 00:03:46,249
One for the clock, one for the "me

120
00:03:46,249 --> 00:03:48,545
time," and one for the finish line.

121
00:03:49,133 --> 00:03:49,958
Fix one.

122
00:03:49,958 --> 00:03:51,983
Set an alarm for going to bed, not

123
00:03:51,983 --> 00:03:52,858
waking up.

124
00:03:52,908 --> 00:03:55,367
Thirty minutes before you want to be asleep.

125
00:03:55,504 --> 00:03:57,404
It's a wind-down reminder.

126
00:03:57,416 --> 00:04:00,154
A homemade stopping cue, since the feed won't

127
00:04:00,154 --> 00:04:01,305
make one for you.

128
00:04:01,430 --> 00:04:03,180
And yes, you'll swipe it away the first

129
00:04:03,180 --> 00:04:03,808
time.

130
00:04:03,942 --> 00:04:04,567
That's fine.

131
00:04:04,567 --> 00:04:05,432
You noticed.

132
00:04:05,763 --> 00:04:06,501
Fix two.

133
00:04:06,501 --> 00:04:08,938
Move some of your "me time" earlier.

134
00:04:08,951 --> 00:04:11,063
Even ten minutes of something just for you

135
00:04:11,063 --> 00:04:12,055
before dinner.

136
00:04:12,225 --> 00:04:13,938
The idea is that if some of the

137
00:04:13,938 --> 00:04:16,750
day is already yours, the night has less

138
00:04:16,750 --> 00:04:17,842
to make up for.

139
00:04:17,985 --> 00:04:19,610
Think of it as a deposit.

140
00:04:19,622 --> 00:04:22,277
A small one, before the late-night withdrawal.

141
00:04:22,454 --> 00:04:24,004
Does it work for everyone?

142
00:04:24,017 --> 00:04:25,067
No promises.

143
00:04:25,067 --> 00:04:27,123
It's something to try, not a cure.

144
00:04:27,302 --> 00:04:29,702
Both are free, which is the correct price

145
00:04:29,702 --> 00:04:31,645
for a person who can't be bothered.

146
00:04:31,795 --> 00:04:33,945
And the third is my favorite, because it

147
00:04:33,945 --> 00:04:35,736
takes barely any discipline.

148
00:04:36,317 --> 00:04:37,042
Fix three.

149
00:04:37,042 --> 00:04:39,092
Decide the last thing of the night before

150
00:04:39,092 --> 00:04:39,942
you start it.

151
00:04:39,954 --> 00:04:41,029
One episode.

152
00:04:41,029 --> 00:04:42,017
One chapter.

153
00:04:42,029 --> 00:04:43,195
One crossword.

154
00:04:43,372 --> 00:04:45,872
The point is deciding in advance.

155
00:04:45,909 --> 00:04:47,997
Not "one more," in the moment, with a

156
00:04:47,997 --> 00:04:49,322
tired brain.

157
00:04:49,347 --> 00:04:51,515
The number gets set while you're awake.

158
00:04:51,669 --> 00:04:53,844
Then the thing ends, the credits roll, and

159
00:04:53,844 --> 00:04:55,719
you get a real stopping cue.

160
00:04:55,744 --> 00:04:57,221
It feels illegal.

161
00:04:57,361 --> 00:04:58,689
Pair it with one rule.

162
00:04:58,820 --> 00:05:00,845
When the last thing finishes, the lights go

163
00:05:00,845 --> 00:05:01,370
out.

164
00:05:01,383 --> 00:05:03,012
No bonus episode.

165
00:05:03,204 --> 00:05:04,954
A small honest note.

166
00:05:04,954 --> 00:05:07,129
If your sleep stays bad even when you

167
00:05:07,129 --> 00:05:09,342
do go to bed, that's worth raising with

168
00:05:09,342 --> 00:05:10,332
a professional.

169
00:05:10,509 --> 00:05:11,122
That's it.

170
00:05:11,122 --> 00:05:13,634
Alarm, deposit, finish line.

171
00:05:13,659 --> 00:05:16,109
Three small things I can barely be bothered

172
00:05:16,109 --> 00:05:16,862
to say.

173
00:05:17,163 --> 00:05:18,888
Remember tomorrow Bob?

174
00:05:18,888 --> 00:05:21,551
The one who wasn't invited, wasn't consulted, and

175
00:05:21,551 --> 00:05:22,992
always gets the bill?

176
00:05:23,161 --> 00:05:24,667
Tonight, you could invite him.

177
00:05:25,392 --> 00:05:26,355
He'll say thanks.

178
00:05:26,355 --> 00:05:28,464
Nobody's ever given him the chance.
